# TODO

1. Planning again from the headers, with only revisions.yml removed rather than every CHANGELOG build wrote. plan cannot tell a CHANGELOG build wrote from one written by hand, both being in changelogger's form once built, so revisions.yml would have to record which revisions' entries came from a header, and plan read those again and keep the rest.
2. Take the binary's name from the CHANGELOG, rather than assuming the sole entry in bin/.
3. Produce a single CHANGELOG in the last version directory, rather than a cumulative one in every directory.
4. Work for git repositories, whether by writing outside the repository or by rewriting history.
5. Take the entry's date from the latest header in the tree, not only the main file's. A revision which changed only some other file is dated from the main file's header, which may be older.
6. Multiple dates in a CHANGELOG heading, as a header's date line already carries them: `## 20260913, 1005`, escalating against the element before it, the last being the completion date.  It fails quietly: `Changelog::Format::DatedHeading`'s `HEADING` and `BOUNDARY` want eight digits exactly, so such a heading begins no entry of its own.  Below the newest, the heading is read as an unversioned entry and the entries beneath it take the date of the heading above; as the newest, it and its entries go with the file's title.  `expand_dates` already reads the escalating form for a header's date line and would serve, the entry taking the last of the dates; and `write_changelog` writes the single `entry[:date]` it holds, so it would have to keep the heading it read rather than reduce it.  Nothing needs this: changelogger writes a single date by design, reducing a multi-date header to its completion date, so only a hand-written CHANGELOG would carry one.
